  "insights": [
   "The US rare earth supply chain remains heavily reliant on China, with critical vulnerabilities in processing, heavy rare earth separation, and magnet manufacturing rather than mining.",
   "While US mining output is growing, the value-added stages (separation, metallisation, magnet production) remain dominated by China, which controls 91% of refined output and 94% of permanent magnet production.",
   "Heavy rare earths like dysprosium and terbium represent the weakest link, as domestic commercial-scale separation is only in the early stages.",
   "US magnet capacity is insufficient to meet domestic demand, and the IEA projects that diversified magnet output outside China will meet well below 20% of non-China demand by 2035.",
   "A critical deadline for the US defense industry is approaching on 1 January 2027, when procurement restrictions on magnets originating from China, North Korea, Russia, or Iran take effect.",
